<h1 class="article-title">New Streets Ceremonially Opened in Liepāja Industrial Park</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>Today, 21 November, newly constructed streets in Liepāja Industrial Park, on the site of the former Liepājas Metalurgs factory, were ceremonially opened with the symbolic cutting of a steel bar and a performance of the song “Dzelzsgriezējs” by Ainars Virga. Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis noted that, just as the ironcutter in the band Līvi’s song cuts through the old world, the city is turning a new page in its industrial history and beginning a greener chapter. “The first new streets are only the beginning, the first step toward gradually returning the former Liepājas Metalurgs site to the city and making it publicly accessible. At the same time, it is important to make it attractive to investors who will establish modern manufacturing enterprises offering well-paid jobs based on smart technologies and the basic principles of the circular economy.”</p><p>The idea of establishing Liepāja Industrial Park was endorsed in a memorandum of intent on 23 August 2018. It stated that the park would ensure efficient use of the historic Liepājas Metalurgs site, contribute positively to the city’s sustainable economic development, increase the number of exporting companies and export volumes, boost direct and indirect tax revenue for the state and municipality, and make rational use of the electrical capacity already available on the site. The memorandum was signed by the Ministry of Economics, the Investment and Development Agency of Latvia, Liepāja City Council, the Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority and SIA FeLM.</p><p>The memorandum was signed on behalf of the Ministry of Economics by the then Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Economics, now Minister of Finance, Arvils Ašeradens. “Liepāja works intelligently, cooperating with businesses and developing projects in close synergy with the government and municipality. Liepāja is a success story. Latvia needs strong businesses and places where they can be built and developed so that we can attract investment in high-value-added activities, create export-capable companies and generate new skilled, well-paid jobs. The newly acquired business areas will encourage economic activity not only in Liepāja but also contribute to Latvia’s overall economic growth.”</p><p>The world is changing, industrial technologies are changing, and attitudes toward the environment are changing. This is not a choice but a necessity.</p><p>Liepāja Mayor Gunārs Ansiņš said: “Liepāja is deliberately moving toward greener, smarter and more sustainable development. We have significantly reduced our dependence on fossil energy and are transforming industry into modern, environmentally friendly and competitive manufacturing. By opening the first new streets in Liepāja Industrial Park today, we are literally and symbolically marking the transition from a legacy of heavy metallurgy to climate-neutral, smart and high-value-added industry. This area will become a place where innovation and green energy meet manufacturing and export capacity, showing that Liepāja does not merely follow the European Green Deal but helps shape it.”</p><p>Recent trends in the global economy show that Europe needs manufacturing centres in various sectors, creating development opportunities for Liepāja as well. Over the next ten years, Liepāja intends to develop a green, high-capacity industrial park on the former Liepājas Metalurgs site. It should become an example of green manufacturing and circular-economy efficiency, creating more than 1,500 new jobs. The world is changing, industrial technologies are changing, and attitudes toward the environment are changing. This is not a choice but a necessity.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ Board Chair Uldis Sesks said: “We are proud of our industrial traditions, but today we are witnessing an important moment of industrial transformation. We are changing so that industry becomes safer and friendlier to people and the environment. Sustainable thinking is embedded in the DNA of the future green Liepāja Industrial Park, with education, innovation and circular-economy principles clearly established. Today Liepāja needs manufacturing facilities that are friendly to the environment and local residents, export-oriented and capable of attracting sufficient intellectual resources.”</p><p>At the end of 2023, the Liepāja SEZ Authority secured the first investments for Liepāja Industrial Park and signed an agreement with the Central Finance and Contracting Agency for EUR 10 million in Recovery and Resilience Facility funding to implement the project “Development of Liepāja Industrial Park Infrastructure, Phase 1”. The project provides for 3,865 metres of electricity networks, eight new transformers and three street sections with utilities, including water supply, domestic and stormwater sewerage and district heating. Approximately 2 kilometres of streets have now been built on Inženieru, Eksporta and Dzelzsgriezēja streets. The total value of the completed construction works is EUR 9.23 million. The works were carried out by SIA Aland and SIA CTB.</p><p>In parallel with the SEZ Authority’s project, the municipality implemented the reconstruction of Meldru Street under the programme “Support for the Development of Public Infrastructure Needed for Business, Promoting the Transition to a Climate-Neutral Economy”.</p><p>Naglu and Martena streets are also expected to be commissioned by the end of the year. In 2026, construction is planned for the intersection and connecting road between Brīvības, Čuguna and Eksporta streets. Savings will allow an additional 289-metre section of Martena Street to be built next year. An application under the EU Cohesion Policy Programme 2021–2027 has also been approved, enabling the demolition of fuel-oil tanks next year and the connection of the newly built streets into a single network.</p><p>Liepāja Industrial Park is being developed by the Liepāja SEZ Authority and the City of Liepāja on the former Liepājas Metalurgs site, between Brīvības, Meldru, Ezermalas, Parka and Zemnieku streets. It borders Lake Liepāja, a specially protected NATURA 2000 nature area. The site is being developed as a modern business environment to attract technologically advanced, innovative companies manufacturing high-value-added products in accordance with the European Green Deal and environmentally friendly circular-economy principles. Under the approved development strategy, the overall project aims to attract more than EUR 275 million in private investment and create at least 1,500 jobs.</p><p>Background:</p><ul><li>2018: The Ministry of Economics, Investment and Development Agency of Latvia, SIA FeLM, Liepāja City Council and Liepāja SEZ Authority signed a memorandum of intent to develop the former Liepājas Metalurgs factory site.</li><li>2019: Consolidation of real estate on the former Liepājas Metalurgs site began.</li><li>2021: A development programme for the former factory site was approved, and an interinstitutional agreement on its implementation was concluded with Liepāja City Council.</li><li>2022: The local spatial plan for the former Liepājas Metalurgs site was approved.</li><li>2022: A remediation programme for the former Liepājas Metalurgs site was prepared.</li><li>2023: The Liepāja Industrial Park (LIP) Development Strategy for 2023–2028 was approved.</li><li>2023: Several construction designs were developed for infrastructure works on the site.</li><li>2023: An application for funding the first street construction works in LIP was prepared and submitted, and a positive funding decision was received.</li><li>2023: A lease agreement with SIA Liepājas Steel was concluded until 31 December 2025 for dismantling and removing the electric steelmaking furnace.</li><li>Autumn 2024: Construction of the first street sections and utility networks, and electrification of the entire site, began.</li><li>2025: Disposal of existing industrial buildings began.</li></ul></div>
